      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm seeing a CrashLoopBackOff on our auth-service microservice in our GKE cluster. i've checked the basic logs but they're pretty sparse. can u give me a diagnostic checklist to figure out if this is a resource limit issue, a liveness/readiness probe failure, or just a bad config? \n\nhere's the snippet from the pod events:\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason     Age                From               Message\n  ----     ------     ----               ----               -------\n  Normal   Scheduled  15m                default-scheduler  Successfully assigned default/auth-service-7f8d to node-1\n  Normal   Pulling    14m                kubelet            Pulling image \"gcr.io/prod-cluster/auth-service:v2.1.4\"\n  Normal   Pulled     14m                kubelet            Successfully pulled image \"gcr.io/prod-cluster/auth-service:v2.1.4\"\n  Normal   Created    14m                kubelet            Created container auth-service\n  Normal   Started    14m                kubelet            Started container auth-service\n  Warning  BackOff    2m (x5 over 10m)   kubelet            Back-off restarting failed container",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A CrashLoopBackOff, especially with sparse logs, is a classic sign of a startup failure. First, check the container's actual exit code and reason using `kubectl describe pod auth-service-7f8d`. This will often tell you *why* the container is immediately dying, which is more informative than just seeing the `BackOff` warning. If the exit code is non-zero, it points to an application error, not necessarily a probe failure.\n\nIf the exit code is 0, or if the logs *still* look empty, then you need to systematically check the resource constraints and probes. Compare your pod's `requests` and `limits` against the node's available capacity. Next, verify your Liveness and Readiness probes are correctly configured, pointing to a valid health endpoint that returns a 2xx status code. A bad configuration, like a wrong port or an endpoint that times out instantly, will cause the kubelet to repeatedly restart the container, leading to this exact state.",
      "source": "target",
